  "selected": {
    "code": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    N = int(data[0]); K = int(data[1]); X = int(data[2])\n    T = list(map(int, data[3:3+N]))\n\n    # prefix sums\n    prefix = [0]*(N+1)\n    for i in range(N):\n        prefix[i+1] = prefix[i] + T[i]\n\n    def cost_segment(p, i, d):\n        # segment indices p..i-1 (0-based), count = i-p\n        cnt = i - p\n        return cnt * d - (prefix[i] - prefix[p])\n\n    # dp[i] = min dissatisfaction to ship first i orders (1-based index)\n    # We need to track the previous shipment day; but we can compute by iterating over segment and choosing d based on previous day.\n    # To handle previous day constraint, we can compute dp[i] = min over p<i, size<=K of dp[p] + best_cost(p,i)\n    # where best_cost depends on previous shipment day from p, not just dp[p].\n    # Since N is small, we do a DP with states dp[i][j]? Better:\n    # Use dp[i] = minimum dissatisfaction for first i orders, and for each i, maintain best previous shipment day?\n    # Simpler: DP over index i with constraint from last shipment time.\n    # We can store best[i] = minimum (dissatisfaction, last_shipment_day) lexicographic? Not sufficient for feasibility.\n\n    # Instead, do DP where state is i (#orders shipped) and the last shipment day needed? Hmm.\n    # Since N<=100, we can do DP over i and store best pair: min dissatisfaction and the latest previous day for each dissatisfaction?\n    # Better approach: iterate over segments with explicit prev day.\n    # Keep best[i] = list of (dissatisfaction, last_day) for prefixes? But N<=100, small number of states.\n\n    # Alternative: DP over i with a function that takes previous shipment day as argument? Let's use dp[i] = min over all schedules for first i: (dissatisfaction, last_day)\n    # For each i, we want the schedule with minimum dissatisfaction; but feasibility of future segments depends on last_day.\n    # We can keep all Pareto-optimal pairs (dissatisfaction, last_day). N<=100, number small.\n\n    from math import inf\n    best = [None]*(N+1)\n    best[0] = [(0, None)]  # no orders shipped; last_day None\n    for i in range(1, N+1):\n        candidates = []\n        for p in range(max(0, i-K), i):\n            seg_len = i - p\n            if seg_len < 1 or seg_len > K:\n                continue\n            maxT = T[i-1]  # since sorted, max T in segment p..i-1 is T[i-1]\n            for dis, prev_day in best[p]:\n                lower = maxT\n                if prev_day is not None:\n                    lower = max(lower, prev_day + X)\n                d = lower\n                cost = cost_segment(p, i, d)\n                new_dis = dis + cost\n                candidates.append((new_dis, d))\n        # keep Pareto-optimal (dis, last_day): lower dis is better; if same dis, higher? For future, larger last_day is worse.\n        # We can keep set of (dis, last_day); keep minimal dis for each last_day? Actually future feasibility depends on last_day.\n        # Keep list and prune: for each state, if there exists another state with dis <= and last_day <=, remove.\n        best[i] = []\n        for dis, day in candidates:\n            dominated = False\n            for d2, day2 in best[i]:\n                if d2 <= dis and day2 <= day:\n                    dominated = True\n                    break\n            if not dominated:\n                best[i].append((dis, day))\n            # prune dominated states after inserting\n            new_list = []\n            for d, day in best[i]:\n                dominated2 = False\n                for d2, day2 in best[i]:\n                    if d2 <= d and day2 <= day:\n                        dominated2 = True\n                        break\n                if not dominated2:\n                    new_list.append((d, day))\n            best[i] = new_list\n\n    ans = inf\n    for dis, day in best[N]:\n        ans = min(ans, dis)\n    print(ans)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()",
